p=r/'help/index.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');section='''<section><h2>Inspect column types defaults and constraints</h2><p>Choose <strong>Database schema</strong>, then kind <strong>column</strong> and state <strong>declared column</strong>. Select a row. The labeled memo starts with declared type, explicit nullability and default; scroll for captured constraints, table declaration identity, source line, SHA256 and generation.</p><p>A column is a named table field. Its type describes the declared representation. A default is a captured expression, and a constraint states a rule. Explicit NULL/NOT NULL records clauses present in that entry. Not declared means absent from the entry; it does not establish effective nullability or a database default. DEFAULT NULL stays separate from explicit NULL. String contents are omitted and expressions are never evaluated.</p><p>Filter by type such as DECIMAL, object name, source, explicit nullability or inline constraint text. Choose constraint/captured constraint for table-level entries. Duplicate tables retain separate column ownership. Unsupported clauses, missing types/defaults and contradictory nullability stay gaps. Source navigation uses existing attached/hash/generation guards. Key membership, reference relationships and deployed enforcement remain unverified.</p><p>Full directions and practice: <code>docs/guides/Database_Columns_Run_and_Test.txt</code>. Elevated developer validation: <code>tools/build-database-columns.cmd</code>.</p></section>''';assert '<h2>Inspect column types defaults and constraints</h2>' not in s;s=s.replace('</main>',section+'</main>');p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
